# ZoFHub — environment configuration TEMPLATE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to ".env" and fill in your REAL values.
#   cp .env.example .env
# NEVER commit your real .env — it is gitignored on purpose. This template
# contains placeholders ONLY (no real keys) and is safe to commit.
# In production (Render/Railway/Fly), set these in the host's dashboard, not here.
#
# Every variable the app reads is listed below. Required ones have no "#";
# optional ones are commented out with their default noted.
# =============================================================================

# ── Server ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=8080
HOST=0.0.0.0
NODE_ENV=development
# Don't auto-open a browser tab when the server starts (also auto-skipped when
# NODE_ENV=production). Set to 1 for headless / server runs.
# NO_OPEN=1

# ZoFHub is ALWAYS a proxy site with proxy-friendly ads — there is no hub /
# Google-AdSense mode. The web proxy is always on; monetization is configured
# entirely via PROXY_AD_SCRIPTS + PROXY_BANNER_TAG (see the Ads section below).

# ── Supabase (optional but recommended) ──────────────────────────────────
# If unset, the catalog falls back to bundled JSON and Community is disabled.
# Get these from: Supabase Dashboard → Project Settings → API Keys.
#
# NOTE: NONE of these ever reach the browser. This app is an Express monolith
# and the browser talks only to this server's own /api routes — there is no
# client-side Supabase SDK anywhere in public/, by design. So the publishable
# key below is used SERVER-SIDE too (as the read-only fallback when no admin key
# is set); it is not injected into any page. A test asserts that no configured
# secret value appears in any HTTP response body.
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
# Public (browser-safe) key — protected by Row Level Security. Supabase now
# labels this the "publishable" key (sb_publishable_…); older projects call it
# the "anon" key (a long eyJ… JWT).
# The app accepts EITHER name — set whichever your dashboard shows. (render.yaml
# uses SUPABASE_ANON_KEY; this file uses SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY — same value.)
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=your-anon-or-publishable-key
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key        # alias for the line above — pick one
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY. Bypasses RLS for cron + writes. NEVER expose to the browser
# and NEVER commit it.
#
# PREFERRED — the new-style secret key from Settings → API Keys (sb_secret_…).
# Supabase is retiring the legacy `service_role` JWT at the end of 2026, and
# legacy keys can no longer be rotated individually: the only way to retire a
# leaked one is to create a secret key, move onto it, then deactivate the legacy
# pair. A secret key bypasses RLS exactly like service_role, so it's a drop-in.
# When both are set this one wins, and it is also mirrored onto
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY so the CLI tools that read that name keep working.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_your-secret-key
# LEGACY fallback — still accepted, still works until Supabase turns it off.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key
# (NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL / _ANON_KEY / _PUBLISHABLE_KEY are also accepted as
#  fallbacks, for configs imported from a Next.js project.)

# ── Leaderboard ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Random 32+ char string. Signs the proof-of-play score tokens. Keep it stable
# across restarts (set a fixed value) so issued tokens stay valid.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
SCORE_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string
# Optional: trim each game's leaderboard to its top N scores (default 200).
# SCORES_KEEP_PER_GAME=200

# ── Admin console ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There are exactly TWO ways to be an administrator, and no third.
#
#   1. A SESSION, for humans — username + password at /admin/login.
#   2. A BEARER TOKEN, for machines — ADMIN_SECRET in an Authorization header.
#
# Generate all three session credentials in one step:
#
#     npm run admin-hash
#
# It prompts for a password with the echo hidden, prints the HASH and a fresh
# session secret, and never writes the password anywhere. Paste the output into
# your host's dashboard (Render → Environment) — preferably not into this file,
# so rotating a credential is not a git commit.
ADMIN_USERNAME=
# scrypt hash produced by `npm run admin-hash`. NEVER a plaintext password.
# Format: scrypt$N$r$p$salt$hash — versioned, so the KDF can be changed later.
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
# Keys the session tokens AND the IP hashes in the audit trail. 32+ chars.
# Changing it signs every admin session out and makes older IP hashes
# incomparable with new ones — which is exactly what you want after a leak.
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=
#
# Optional session tuning (defaults shown):
# ADMIN_SESSION_MAX_MS=28800000     # absolute lifetime, 8h
# ADMIN_SESSION_IDLE_MS=1800000     # idle timeout, 30m
# ADMIN_LOCK_THRESHOLD=5            # failures from one IP before lockout
# ADMIN_LOCK_WINDOW_MS=900000       # window those failures are counted in
# ADMIN_LOCK_MS=900000              # how long the lockout lasts
#
# ROTATING THE PASSWORD: run `npm run admin-hash` again and replace
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH. Existing sessions stay valid until they expire, so if the
# old password may be known, also press "Sign out everywhere" in the Security
# Centre — that revokes every session server-side, immediately.
#
# LOCKED OUT? The credentials live only in the environment, so there is nothing
# to reset in the database: set a new ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH in your host's
# dashboard and redeploy. ADMIN_SECRET below is the independent break-glass path
# to the admin API while you do it.
#
# MACHINE TOKEN — for uptime monitors and CLI tooling that cannot hold a cookie.
# Sent as `Authorization: Bearer <value>` or the `x-admin-secret` header.
# It is NOT accepted in a query string: a credential in a URL is written to the
# hosting access log, kept in browser history, and forwarded in the Referer of
# every outbound link on the page.
# Auth FAILS CLOSED — while every admin credential is unset, /api/admin/* returns
# 503 and flagged scores are neither reviewable nor auto-deleted; they just
# accumulate. Same generator as SCORE_SECRET above; use a DIFFERENT value.
ADMIN_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# ── Ads (proxy-friendly networks) ────────────────────────────────────────
# ZoFHub uses proxy-friendly ad networks ONLY (Adsterra, HilltopAds, Monetag, …).
# Google AdSense is banned on proxy sites and is auto-stripped everywhere, so it
# is never used. Paste your network's page-level loader-script URL(s) here,
# comma-separated (e.g. an Adsterra popunder + a Monetag push tag). https only;
# any Google/AdSense URL is rejected. Leave blank to run ad-free.
PROXY_AD_SCRIPTS=
# ── Fixed display slots ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Three independent banner positions. Each takes the RAW tag your network gives
# you (usually two <script> tags) on ONE line, and each renders ONLY when set —
# an unset slot never appears, so there are no empty "Advertisement" boxes.
# Google banner code is refused in all three (a Google ad on a proxy domain is an
# account-LEVEL ban). Every slot reserves its exact size up-front, so a late ad
# can never shift the page (no layout jump).
#
# START WITH ONE NETWORK. Stacking several popunder networks makes them fight
# each other and trips their fraud checks — the fastest way to get banned by all
# of them and paid by none. Add the second only once the first is paying.
#
# Footer banner — 728×90 desktop / 320×50 mobile, at the very bottom.
# PROXY_BANNER_TAG=<script>atOptions={...}</script><script src="https://network/invoke.js"></script>
#
# Header leaderboard — 728×90 desktop / 320×50 mobile, directly under the header.
# PROXY_HEADER_TAG=<script>atOptions={...}</script><script src="https://network/invoke.js"></script>
#
# Sidebar rectangle — 300×250, inside the category sidebar. Hidden below 900px
# wide, where the sidebar collapses and a 300px block would squeeze the grid.
# PROXY_SIDEBAR_TAG=<script src="https://network/tag.js"></script>
# Optional extra ads.txt lines (e.g. an MCM partner), \n-separated.
# ADS_TXT_EXTRA=
# Extra ad origins for the Content-Security-Policy. USUALLY NOT NEEDED — the
# origins of whatever you set in PROXY_AD_SCRIPTS / PROXY_BANNER_TAG are
# allowlisted automatically (plus a wildcard for each registrable domain).
# Set this only when a network chains to a DIFFERENT domain and the browser
# console shows "Refused to load the script 'https://…'". Without an allowlist
# entry the browser blocks the script outright, which looks identical to the
# network simply not paying out. Comma-separated, wildcards OK, https only,
# Google ad domains refused. Check with: npm run check-ads
# PROXY_AD_ORIGINS=https://*.someadcdn.com

# ── Legal pages (Privacy / Terms / DMCA / Cookies) ───────────────────────
# Contact shown on the legal pages. If you leave CONTACT_EMAIL blank, the pages
# link to your GitHub repo's Issues page instead. A dedicated inbox is strongly
# recommended for ad-network approval and DMCA notices.
CONTACT_EMAIL=
# Your public site URL, shown in the policy text (e.g. https://zofhub.com).
# If blank, the policies just say "this website".
SITE_URL=

# ── AI (Pollinations) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO KEY, NO SIGNUP, NO COST. The AI chat calls Pollinations' free + keyless
# endpoints DIRECTLY from each visitor's browser:
#   • Text:  https://text.pollinations.ai/…          (openai-fast model)
#   • Image: https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/…  (<img> — works everywhere)
# So AI load never hits this server and there's nothing to configure to make it
# work. (The newer gen.pollinations.ai endpoints now REQUIRE a paid key, so we
# deliberately do NOT use them.)
#
# ONE caveat, dev-only: Pollinations' Cloudflare Turnstile blocks the TEXT fetch
# from http/localhost origins (it keys on the Origin header). On a real https
# domain the keyless text URL works browser-direct; on localhost the app falls
# back to its own /api/ai/text hop so the chat still works while you build. If
# every network route fails, an on-device model answers as a last resort, so the
# chat never hard-fails. Images (via <img>) send no Origin and work everywhere.
#
# Your public site hostname — sent as the referrer for attribution. No secret.
# Use a BARE hostname (no https://); the server adds the scheme. A full URL with
# a scheme is also tolerated (the code strips it), but bare is the canonical form.
POLLINATIONS_REFERRER=your-domain.example
# Optional FREE public key (pk_…) from https://enter.pollinations.ai — lifts the
# anonymous per-IP rate limit. Handed to the browser on purpose, so it must be a
# PUBLIC pk_ key; never put a secret sk_ key here.
# POLLINATIONS_PUBLIC_KEY=
# Optional Pollinations token to lift the per-IP limit (keep secret if set).
# POLLINATIONS_TOKEN=
# Same thing under the name Pollinations' current docs use. If BOTH are set this
# one wins (server.js prefers it), and either one switches the server-side path
# onto the keyed gen.pollinations.ai endpoint. A secret — never a pk_ key.
# POLLINATIONS_API_KEY=
# Optional paid video-generation provider key (Replicate / fal.ai / Luma /
# Runway). Until set, /api/ai/video returns "unavailable" (no free video model).
# VIDEO_API_KEY=
#
# ── AI_SERVER_FALLBACK — READ THIS BEFORE SETTING IT ─────────────────────
# DO NOT SET THIS IN PRODUCTION. It is the single switch that turns this
# server into an AI proxy, which the entire architecture exists to prevent.
#
# Normally /api/ai/* answers ONLY for localhost and ONLY outside production, so
# the localhost Turnstile caveat above stays a development convenience. Setting
# this to 1 removes that gate everywhere: visitor prompts and AI responses would
# then flow through this host, land in its access logs, and bill its bandwidth —
# and the privacy claim that AI traffic never touches the server stops being
# true. Left unset, the browser talks to Pollinations directly.
# AI_SERVER_FALLBACK=1

# ── Advanced tuning (all optional; defaults are in the code) ─────────────
# Extra origins allowed to make cross-origin WRITE requests, comma-separated.
# Reads are open (the catalog is public data); writes are same-origin by default.
# Only add an origin you control — each one can drive write endpoints using its
# own visitors' IPs.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://your-other-site.example
#
# Token-bucket rate limits. CAPACITY is the burst size, REFILL_MS the interval at
# which one token returns.
# AI_RATE_CAPACITY=              # burst allowance for /api/ai/*
# AI_RATE_REFILL_MS=
# WRITE_RATE_CAPACITY=           # burst allowance for write endpoints
# WRITE_RATE_REFILL_MS=
#
# AI pipeline behaviour (lib/ai-pipeline.mjs).
# AI_MODEL_PRIORITY=             # comma-separated model names, best first
# AI_TOTAL_BUDGET_MS=            # wall-clock ceiling for one AI request
# AI_RETRY_ATTEMPTS=
# AI_RETRY_BASE_MS=              # exponential backoff base
# AI_SIDELINE_AFTER=             # consecutive failures before a model is benched
# AI_HEALTH_COOLDOWN_MS=         # how long a benched model stays benched
#
# Weather grounding (lib/weather.mjs).
# WEATHER_TTL_MS=                # cache lifetime for a forecast
# WEATHER_TIMEOUT_MS=            # upstream request timeout
#
# Housekeeping.
# REC_KEEP_MAX=                  # rows the recommendations cron retains
# SKIP_THUMB_FETCH=1             # skip thumbnail backfill at boot (faster start)
#
# Test-suite ports — set only if 3399/3398 collide with something local.
# TEST_PORT=3399
# TEST_PORT_SEC=3398
# CHECK_ADS_BASE=http://localhost:8080   # base URL `npm run check-ads` probes

# ── Optional: Redis cache (falls back to in-memory if unset) ─────────────
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
# UPSTASH_REDIS_URL=            # alias — used only if REDIS_URL is unset

# ── Optional: cron control ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# CRON_DISABLED=1   # disable all background jobs (useful for tests / replicas)
# Y8_PAGES=0        # 0 = scrape ALL pages of the Y8 embed directory

# ── Keep-alive (free-tier uptime) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Protects GET /healthz (and /ping). Point an external uptime monitor
# (e.g. cron-job.org) at /healthz every ~10 min so Render's free tier never
# sleeps; the endpoint also pokes Supabase so that free-tier DB never pauses.
# Your monitor must send this value as one of:
#   Authorization: Bearer <secret>   |   x-cron-secret: <secret>   |   ?key=<secret>
# Generate a strong random value:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
CRON_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# ── Advanced: companion proxy browsers ───────────────────────────────────
# ZoFHub can list separately-run standalone proxy browsers in the /browser
# engine picker. These are OPTIONAL and NOT bundled (see CREDITS.md) — point
# them at the services only if you actually run them; leave unset to hide them.
# Setting any ONE of these is enough to make that engine appear in the dropdown
# (whenever it's reachable) — the server is the single source of truth, and the
# browser page lists whatever /api/proxies returns. `npm run dev:all` sets all
# four automatically, so for local use you normally don't need to touch these.
# Defaults shown are the bundled-launcher ports.
# PROXY_NAUTILUS_URL=http://localhost:8091/
# PROXY_INTERSTELLAR_URL=http://localhost:8092/
# PROXY_DAYDREAM_URL=http://localhost:8093/
# PROXY_NEBULA_URL=http://localhost:8094/
#
# NOTE: these are LOCAL-ONLY unless you deploy each companion as its own web
# service. They're whole standalone apps that assume they own the site root, so
# they cannot be mounted under a ZoFHub sub-path. On the live deploy none of
# these are set, so /browser shows the three built-in same-origin engines
# (Scramjet · Ultraviolet · GUST). To get a companion live, deploy it somewhere
# and set its URL here to that public address — no code change needed.
# Matching *_PORT variants (PROXY_NAUTILUS_PORT, PROXY_INTERSTELLAR_PORT,
# PROXY_DAYDREAM_PORT, PROXY_NEBULA_PORT) are also read by the bundled launcher.
